  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Popular education, advocacy and campaigning on themes of Economic Justice</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The geographical location of CIEPAC&#x2019;s work in Chiapas, on the southern border of Mexico, is strategic because of the increasing presence of transnational corporations in the region. Here the attraction is the abundance of relatively unexploited natural resources - oil, water, minerals and timber &#x2013; and the rich biodiversity. Nevertheless, political and social factors do not make it easy for transnationals to operate, as a result of the Zapatista movement that has been fighting, since the introduction of NAFTA in 1994, for greater indigenous autonomy and control over the social and economic development of the region. Since the Zapatista uprising, Chiapas has been heavily militarised, causing resistance by several sectors and reasons to fear for the security of social leaders and movements. In Chiapas, especially in the region Los Altos, there is huge polarisation amongst social actors (state and federal governments, EZLN, JBG, NGOs, political parties, churches, municipal bodies, government officers from different levels, campesino organisations, etc.). The majority of the population is not taken into account when decisions are made.

Within this complex mix of political conflict, social revolution and untapped economic wealth, CIEPAC&#x2019;s educational and campaigning work was well placed to provide a unique example of linkage between local and global development issues. CIEPAC aimed to strengthen the voice of social movements in Chiapas, Mexico and Latin America in promoting policies that reduce levels of poverty and injustice, through information, education, mobilising and networking towards alternatives for a more human and inclusive world. 

CIEPAC closed as an organisation in 2011.</narrative>
  </description>
